  node [
    id 128
    label "CsG5YcN4BBc"
    step 0
    date "2017-06-07T14:47:30Z"
    title "How This Barber With Tourettes Syndrome Overcomes Twitches and Tics | New York Post"
    description "This Brooklyn barber has shear willpower. David Arce, 28, has Tourette&#8217;s syndrome &#8212; a neurological condition that makes his arms twitch involuntarily, forcing him occasionally to brace himself on the chair as a tic flares up.&#10;&#10;#barber #tourettes #syndrome #condition #disorder #neurology &#10;&#10;From 'How this barber with Tourette&#8217;s overcomes twitches to live his dream' - https://nypost.com/2017/06/06/how-this-barber-with-tourettes-overcomes-twitches-to-live-his-dream/&#10;&#10;Spend a couple minutes in David Arce&#8217;s chair and you&#8217;ll ask the same question as every other customer: &#8220;What&#8217;s that shaking?&#8221;&#10;&#10;It&#8217;s such a common question that Arce has a sign on his station that says, &#8220;Why do I act this way? I can&#8217;t control it.&#8221;&#10;&#10;He is the first to admit that he made an odd choice for a profession, but Arce has been cutting hair for 14 years, since he was a teen &#8212; and says that the work actually makes his condition more bearable.&#10;&#10;&#8220;I don&#8217;t tic as much when I&#8217;m cutting hair or shaving someone,&#8221; said Arce, who can experience tics up to 12 times a minute.&#10;&#10;A customer once berated him in front of a full shop.&#10;&#10;But Arce has a way of turning most of his first-timers into return customers, according to Sean LeVan, who got the &#8220;hipster high and tight&#8221; his first time in the barber&#8217;s chair.&#10;&#10;Arce has shown symptoms of Tourette&#8217;s syndrome, which can also cause involuntary shaking and verbal outbursts, since he was 8 years old, but the condition went undiagnosed for more than a decade.&#10;&#10;At 14, he decided to follow in his cosmetologist mother&#8217;s footsteps.&#10;&#10;As a teen, his condition made it tough to find typical jobs, and he began working part-time with his mom at a salon in Bensonhurst.&#10;&#10;Arce spent three years at the salon honing his craft &#8212; without a license, but under his mom&#8217;s watch, he said. The work gave people a new way to define him &#8212; aside from his Tourette&#8217;s.&#10;&#10;But Arce needed to strike out on his own to prove he could succeed.&#10;&#10;A patron once likened him to Sweeney Todd, the fictional murderous Victorian coiffeur who dispatched his clients with a swift stroke of his straight razor.&#10;&#10;In the ensuing years, he went from Brooklyn joints to upscale establishments, including the city&#8217;s oldest barbershop, Paul Mole on the Upper East Side and the Lower East Side&#8217;s hip Frank&#8217;s Chop Shop, earning him a loyal customers and thousands of Instagram followers.&#10;&#10;Arce is so deft a barber that the owners of Frank&#8217;s &#8212; known for high-profile clients such as Jake Gyllenhaal and Wu Tang Clan&#8217;s GZA &#8212; never felt the need to ask him about his obvious twitching.&#10;&#10;Arce eventually landed at Tomcats Barbershop in Greenpoint, where the clientele is a mix of fashionable and easygoing that works for him, he said.&#10;&#10;Tourette&#8217;s and related tic disorders affect one in every 100 kids, and there is no cure, according to the Tourette Association of America, an advocacy group that is currently marking Tourette&#8217;s Awareness Month from May 15 to June 15.&#10; &#10;Some sufferers are compelled to blurt things out, and others endure physical twitches. Arce is among the latter &#8212; spasms originate in his torso and sometimes reverberate along his arms, earning him the nickname &#8220;Twitch&#8221; among friends, he said.&#10;&#10;A patron once likened him to Sweeney Todd, the fictional murderous Victorian coiffeur who dispatched his clients with a swift stroke of his straight razor and was made famous in the dark Stephen Sondheim musical.&#10;&#10;That customer was scheduled for a straight-razor shave, but Arce sensed the tension and just used the clippers instead.&#10;&#10;The New York Post is your source for breaking news, news about New York, sports, business, entertainment, opinion, real estate, culture, fashion, and more.&#10;&#10;Catch the latest news and gossip here: &#10;https://nypost.com/video/&#10;Follow The New York Post on:&#10;Twitter - https://twitter.com/nypost&#10;Facebook - https://www.facebook.com/NYPost"
    channeltitle "New York Post"
